Applications and Advantages
- Preliminary wine-quality screening based on measured chemical
  properties.
- Comparing batches and observing patterns in physicochemical
  measurements.
- Identifying which features the trained model relies on.
- Practising an end-to-end machine-learning workflow with both
  regression and classification.
Limitations
- The target quality value is a sensory score.
- Quality categories are imbalanced in the source data.
- The dataset does not provide details such as brand, price, or grape
  variety.
- Outliers were explored but not explicitly removed in the recorded
  workflow.
- Results depend on the selected train-test split and model settings.
- Feature importance describes model reliance; it does not establish
  causation.
Future Scope
- Use cross-validation and systematic hyperparameter tuning.
- Investigate class weighting or resampling for minority classes.
- Evaluate additional ensemble and gradient-boosting models.
- Validate performance on new, independent wine samples.
- Build a simple web interface or API for predictions.
Conclusion
The project demonstrates how machine learning can use physicochemical
measurements to estimate wine quality in two complementary ways:
predicting a numerical score through regression and assigning a Low,
Medium, or High category through classification. The workflow covers
data preparation, exploratory analysis, model training, evaluation,
model comparison, confusion-matrix analysis, feature-importance
analysis, and sample prediction. The results should be interpreted as a
decision-support prototype and not as a replacement for expert wine
assessment.
